Let me carefully design all function names to avoid collisions.

### Part 1 content

```js
/* ============================================================
   REALITY FORGE
   ...
   ============================================================ */
import * as THREE from 'three';
import * as CANNON from 'cannon-es';
```
Wait: static imports will fail and prevent any error handling. Use dynamic:
```js
let THREE, CANNON;
try {
  THREE = await import('three');
  CANNON = await import('cannon-es');
} catch (err) { showFatal(...); throw err; }
```
But functions like `new THREE.Vector3` at top-level after await are fine. However, if import fails, the rest shouldn't run. Wrap init in `main()`. Structure:

```js
const loadErrorEl = ...
let THREE, CANNON;
try {...} catch (e) { ... }
if (THREE && CANNON) main();
```
Top-level await allowed in module. Use:
```js
let THREE, CANNON;
try {
  [THREE, CANNON] = await Promise.all([import('three'), import('cannon-es')]);
} catch (e) {
  document.getElementById('loadError').classList.add('on');
  ...
  throw e;
}
```
Then all code. If import fails, throw and nothing else runs. Good.

