No global module-load error handling. Add a small classic script before the module in shell.html that sets a timeout (e.g., 12s) and shows an error if `window.__READY__` isn't true, plus listens for `error` events on script loading. This is a nice robustness improvement for CDN failures/offline.

But careful: module scripts execute after parsing; if import fails, `window.__READY__` never set. Add:

```html
<script>
(function(){
  var timer = setTimeout(function(){
    if (window.__READY__) return;
    var box = document.getElementById('loadError');
    var sub = document.querySelector('#loading .load-sub');
    if (sub) sub.textContent = 'CONNECTION SLOW';
    if (box) { box.classList.add('on'); box.textContent = 'Still loading the 3D engine… check your connection. Three.js loads from a CDN.'; }
  }, 12000);
  window.addEventListener('error', function(e){
    if (window.__READY__) return;
    if (e && e.target && e.target.tagName === 'SCRIPT') {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      var box = document.getElementById('loadError');
      var sub = document.querySelector('#loading .load-sub');
      if (sub) sub.textContent = 'ENGINE OFFLINE';
      if (box) { box.classList.add('on'); box.textContent = 'Could not load Three.js from the CDN. Reconnect and reload.'; }
    }
  }, true);
  window.addEventListener('__FORGE_READY__', function(){ clearTimeout(timer); });
})();
</script>
```

But __READY__ set in loop, no event. The timeout checks window.__READY__ anyway; if ready before 12s, it returns. Fine.

Also add `window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection')`? Dynamic import failure is a module script load failure → error event on script element? For module imports, network failures fire error on window? The `<script type=module>` inline executes; static imports failing produce an error event on window? Not reliably. The timeout covers it.

Where to insert: before importmap or after? Classic script can go anywhere before module. Put right after the importmap, before the module script.

Also consider adding a "Retry" button? Keep simple.

Another robustness: the requirement says "Display a subtle non-blocking loading indicator ('Materializing...') while the AI processes the request." We have #materializing. Verified? The prompt flow screenshot didn't capture it, but code calls setMaterializing. Let me verify text and that it shows during local forge (local is instant, so barely visible). For API calls it shows. Good.

Another requirement: "settings gear icon on mobile so I can paste the key directly on my phone" — present.

"microphone button next to the text bar using Web Speech API" — present with graceful fallback. Check that on unsupported browsers it shows a toast and doesn't break. Let me read 06-ui 130-175.

Also API error fallback: if API fails (bad key/network), does it fall back to local? Check 02-ai.js generate flow. Important for usability. Let me read the callSpec logic.

